{"name":"Blue Jeans","hex":"#5dadec","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue jeans","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pair of trousers made from denim cotton and dyed dark blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_jeans"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Jeans (Lana Del Rey song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Blue Jeans\" is a song by American singer-songwriter Lana Del Rey and the third single from her second studio album, Born to Die (2012). Interscope Records released the track to Italian radios on March 30, 2012. Del Rey wrote it with Dan Heath and producer Emile Haynie. The song combines sadcore, trip hop, and gothic pop over a balladic production."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4e/LanaBlueJeans.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Jeans_(Lana_Del_Rey_song)"},{"title":"Blue Jeans (1975 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Jeans is a 1975 Italian erotic-drama film directed by Mario Imperoli and starring Gloria Guida."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b2/Blue_Jeans_%281975_film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Jeans_(1975_film)"},{"title":"Jeans","blocks":[{"text":"Jeans are a type of trousers made from denim or dungaree cloth. Often the term \"jeans\" refers to a particular style of trousers, called \"blue jeans\", with the addition of copper pocket rivets added by Jacob W. Davis in 1871 and patented by Davis and Levi Strauss on May 20, 1873. Prior to the patent, the term \"blue jeans\" had been long in use for various items of workwear, constructed from a heavy blue-colored denim fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Jeans.jpg/330px-Jeans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeans"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Blue_jeans_fading.png/500px-Blue_jeans_fading.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Blue_jeans_fading.png/1280px-Blue_jeans_fading.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Blue_jeans_fading.png/500px-Blue_jeans_fading.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Microscopic image of faded fabric","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_jeans_fading.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Manifattura_genovese%2C_abito_da_festa_in_tela_di_genova_%28jeans%29%2C_1850-1900_ca..JPG/500px-Manifattura_genovese%2C_abito_da_festa_in_tela_di_genova_%28jeans%29%2C_1850-1900_ca..JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Manifattura_genovese%2C_abito_da_festa_in_tela_di_g